Q: I can&#39;t get a check mark to display using a custom toggle style in an inner ForEach View A: <p>When the flag <code>isChecked</code> is toggled on an item, this does not trigger a change to the array holding the item (only the item in the array is changed, not the array itself). So listeners are not notified of the change.</p>
<p>To fix, I would suggest the following changes:</p>
<ol>
<li><p>Add <code>@Observable</code> to <code>ListItem</code>.</p>
</li>
<li><p>Conform <code>ListItem</code> and <code>CategorizedItem</code> to <code>Identifiable</code>. These classes already have an <code>id</code> property, so the protocol is already implemented.</p>
</li>
<li><p>Annotate the instance of <code>ViewModel</code> with <code>@State</code> and make it <code>private</code>.</p>
</li>
<li><p>Change the <code>ForEach</code> to iterate over bindings to the underlying objects, instead of iterating over the array indices.</p>
</li>
<li><p>Supply the toggle with a direct binding to the underlying flag.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<p>Once these changes are made, the functions <code>fetchIsChecked</code> and <code>toggleChecked</code> in <code>ViewModel</code> are no longer needed.</p>
<p>Here is how the updates can be applied:</p>
<pre class="lang-swift prettyprint-override"><code>@Observable class ListItem: Identifiable {
    // ...
}

class CategorizedItem: Identifiable {
    // ...
}

struct ContentView: View {
    @State private var vm: ViewModel = ViewModel()

    var body: some View {
        List{
            VStack (alignment: .leading) {
                // &quot;OUTER&quot; ForEach
                ForEach($vm.activeList) { $categorizedItem in
                    VStack(alignment: .leading) {
                        Text(&quot;\(categorizedItem.category)&quot;).font(.title)
                        VStack(alignment: .leading){
                            // &quot;INNER&quot; ForEach
                            ForEach($categorizedItem.items) { $item in
                                HStack{
                                    Toggle(&quot;&quot;, isOn: $item.isChecked)
                                        .toggleStyle(CheckboxToggleStyle())
                                    Text(item.description)
                                }
                            }
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
</code></pre>

Q: Document a Vue3 Generic Component such that typedoc can list, $emits, $props and $slots A: <p>With the help of &quot;Gemini 3 Pro&quot; I was able to create the following type definition.</p>
<pre><code>// TGenericVue3Component.ts
import type { ComponentPublicInstance, SlotsType, EmitsOptions } from &quot;vue&quot;;

/**
 * Helper type for defining the return type of a Generic Vue Component constructor.
 * Ensures TypeDoc picks up properties (via Readonly&lt;P&gt;) and Vue integration (via ComponentPublicInstance).
 * 
 * Usage:
 * export default {} as unknown as new &lt;T&gt;(props: Props&lt;T&gt;) =&gt; TGVue3GenericComponent&lt;Props&lt;T&gt;&gt;;
 * @group Types
 * @category Types
 */
export type TGenericVue3Component&lt;TProps, TEmits extends EmitsOptions = {}, TSlots extends Record&lt;string, any&gt; = {}&gt; = ComponentPublicInstance&lt;TProps, {}, {}, {}, {}, TEmits, TProps, {}, false, any, {}, SlotsType&lt;TSlots&gt;&gt; &amp; Readonly&lt;TProps&gt; &amp; {
    $props: TProps;
    $slots: TSlots;
    $emits: TEmits;
};
</code></pre>
<h2>Usage</h2>
<p>You have to cast the <code>export default {}</code> to <code>unknown</code> first, then you case it again to a contructor definition which outputs TGenericVue3Component with your props, emits and slots-interfaces.</p>
<pre class="lang-ts prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;script lang=&quot;ts&quot;&gt;
...
/**
 * ...
 * @class
 */
export default defineComponent({}) as unknown as new &lt;T&gt;(props: IGVue3DropdownProps&lt;T&gt;) =&gt; TGenericVue3Component&lt;typeof props, IGVue3DropdownEmits&lt;T&gt;, IGVue3DropdownSlots&lt;T&gt;&gt;;
&lt;/script&gt;
&lt;script setup lang=&quot;ts&quot; generic=&quot;TId extends TListEntryPropertyName, TData&quot;&gt;
...
&lt;/script&gt;
</code></pre>
<h2>How it displays</h2>
<p>This is nicely picked up by typedoc like:
<img src="https://i.sstatic.net/9QI0vogK.png" alt="Typedoc nicely formatted properties etc." /></p>
<p>and VS-Code typehints
<img src="https://i.sstatic.net/5BbUIbHO.png" alt="VS-Code typehints on hover" /></p>

Q: How can I display Edittext and checkbox input (+showing checkbox labels) into a recyclerview (using 2 activities, 1 for input, 1 for display) A: <p><strong>(Side note: There was a previous answer here, SIMILAR to what I've got here in this answer):</strong></p>
<h1>For the fix (Recycler item display + Checkbox labels)</h1>
<p>I added in the <code>Home_Page_Activity</code>: a ActivityResult Launcher, think this helps with home page to keep the list so instead of creating a new activity that might refresh the list. Especially when it came from <code>startActivity(intent)</code>. <em>(from memory, this was optional)</em></p>
<pre><code>public class Home_Page_Activity extends AppCompatActivity {

    public ActivityResultLauncher&lt;Intent&gt; ARLauncher;

     ARLauncher = registerForActivityResult(new ActivityResultContracts.StartActivityForResult(), result -&gt; {if (result.getResultCode() == RESULT_OK) {
            Habit_Data_Model habitDataModel = (Habit_Data_Model) result.getData().getSerializableExtra(&quot;List&quot;);
           habit_list.add(habitDataModel);
            habitAdapter = new Habit_Adapter(habit_list);

            Habit_listRV.setLayoutManager(new LinearLayoutManager(Home_Page_Activity.this));

            Habit_listRV.setAdapter(habitAdapter);
           habitAdapter.notifyItemInserted(habit_list.size() -1);
        }});
}
</code></pre>
<p>Then added this in the <code>Add_Habit_button</code> click method</p>
<pre><code>            ARLauncher.launch(intent);
</code></pre>
<p>For the <code>Add_Habit_Activity</code> I've replaced <code>startActivity(intent)</code> with the code below:</p>
<pre><code>            setResult(RESULT_OK, intent);
            finish();
</code></pre>
<h2>For the checkboxes labels</h2>
<p>I've added this in the xml file. A new TextView for displaying the habit frequency.</p>
<ul>
<li><h3>habit_layout.xml</h3>
</li>
</ul>
<pre><code>&lt;TextView
    android:id=&quot;@+id/HL_Habit_frequency&quot;
    android:layout_width=&quot;match_parent&quot;
    android:layout_height=&quot;wrap_content&quot;
    android:layout_marginTop=&quot;5dp&quot;
    android:layout_marginEnd=&quot;100dp&quot;
    android:layout_marginStart=&quot;40dp&quot;&gt;
&lt;/TextView&gt;
</code></pre>
<h3></h3>
<ul>
<li><h3><strong>Habit Data Model Class</strong></h3>
</li>
</ul>
<p>Firstly, I've added a field named <code>habit_frequency</code> in the <code>Habit_Data_Model</code> class:</p>
<pre><code>    public String habit_frequency;// For the checkbox labels 
</code></pre>
<p>Then added this in the <code>Habit_Data_Model</code> 's constructor</p>
<pre><code>this.habit_frequency = habit_frequency;
</code></pre>
<p>And then added this block of code below in the same <code>Habit_Data_Model</code> class:</p>
<pre><code>public String getfrequency(){
    ArrayList&lt;String&gt; habit_freq_list = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();
    if(checkbox_value_daily) habit_freq_list.add(&quot;Daily&quot;);
    if(checkbox_value_monthly) habit_freq_list.add(&quot;Monthly&quot;);
    if(checkbox_value_weekly) habit_freq_list.add(&quot;Weekly&quot;);
    if(checkbox_value_yearly) habit_freq_list.add(&quot;Yearly&quot;);
    return android.text.TextUtils.join(&quot;, &quot;, habit_freq_list);
}
</code></pre>
<p>This is for each checkbox. If the checkbox is checked (evaluates to true) then add a string to the string ArrayList of whichever checkbox was ticked then return that same list with each item being seperated by a delimiter.</p>
<ul>
<li><h3><strong>Habit Adapter Class</strong></h3>
</li>
</ul>
<p>In the <code>Habit_Adapter</code> I've added this in the <code>onBindViewHolder</code> method:</p>
<pre><code>holder.habit_frequency.setText(habitDataModel.getfrequency());
</code></pre>
<p>Added a new textview variable named <code>habit_frequency</code></p>
<pre><code>TextView habit_name, habit_desc, habit_notes, habit_frequency
</code></pre>
<p>Then added a R.id reference for the variable</p>
<pre><code>habit_frequency = itemView.findViewById(R.id.HL_Habit_frequency);
</code></pre>
<p><em>That's about it, Hope this helps for others too :3</em></p>
